# Emery Plugin

## Overview

This plugin provides the two Emery skills: generate a specification, and build its plan. Each skill is an ultrathin wrapper that invokes the matching `emery` CLI verb and relays its output.

The v1 delivery workflow (plan / refine / execute / status / finalize and the `system-*` definition loop) is archived at git tag `v1`. Its verbs are deleted from the CLI grammar and its skills are deleted from this plugin — do not attempt to run them or reconstruct their behavior by hand. `emery specify` generates `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` from the sources named on the invocation; `emery show spec`, `emery show design`, and `emery show plan` print the reviewable documents of the current revision; `emery build` hands every slice of the current plan, in build order, to one target adapter that builds it into the project tree.

## Workflow

```text
/emery:specify <adapter>           (every revision)
/emery:build [<adapter>]           (once the plan is reviewed)
```

That is the whole shipped surface. An `<adapter>` is an exact package reference with a version, `namespace:name@version` (`emery:typescript@0.13.0`); the binary reads it from its store, `~/.emery/adapters`, and fetches a release the store lacks on the first run that names it. The source list never persists — repeat the adapters on every run, or keep them in an operator-owned `emery.toml` selected with `--config [<path>]` (`--config` alone means the project-relative `emery.toml`; a run naming no sources at all discovers the project-root file as a fallback). Every run starts from its sources alone — nothing of the stored revision reaches the synthesis, and requirements number from `REQ-001` in source order. After each run the skill writes `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` (the Markdown projections) beside the code for review. Never hand-edit `.emery/storage` state (the revision store); route through the CLI.

`/emery:build` names one target adapter the same way, or takes it from the `[target]` table of the same `emery.toml`. It builds the committed plan's slices in build order — the plan's waves flattened, each slice after the slices it depends on — through one adapter dispatch each, then commits the files the build reported written, and only those, with the revision and the slices in the commit message. The tree is the build's output: `emery build` writes no state of its own, and re-running builds every slice again over the tree as it stands. The projections stay `/emery:specify`'s to write, and the build never commits them.

## Tracing and output

One tracing level governs a run — the runtime's host console, the engine, and every adapter alike. A bare run is `info`: the engine's progress at its slow steps and each adapter's seam turn as it opens reach stderr, retries at WARN. Each `-v` raises the level one step and each `-q` lowers it — `-q` warn (progress gone, warnings kept), `-qq` error, `-qqq` off; `-v` debug, `-vv` trace — before or after the verb, and `-v` beside `-q` is a usage error. The flags override a process `RUST_LOG`; a bare run keeps one that is set, so `RUST_LOG=emery_sdk=debug emery specify …` refines a bare run and `RUST_LOG=off` silences it.

**Debug mode**: when the operator explicitly asks for debug (says "debug", or passes `debug` on the slash command), pass `-v` to **every** `emery` subprocess in that skill invocation. A subprocess that must be fully silent passes `-qqq` (or keeps `RUST_LOG=off`) so it does not inherit a raised filter.

Stream roles: the semantic CLI result (text or JSON envelope) is stdout; command failures and live tracing are stderr. Relay the semantic result or structured error once, verbatim; do not repeat or reformat tracing lines in the closing response.

## Troubleshooting

### Common error codes

Every CLI failure prints a kebab-case error code (and usually a `hint:` line — follow it). The codes met most often:

| Error code | What it means | Next action |
|------------|---------------|-------------|
| `specify-source-required` | `emery specify` was run without naming a source, and no project-root `emery.toml` was found | `emery specify <adapter>` (and/or `--description <adapter>=<text>`, or `--config [<path>]`) |
| `build-target-required` | `emery build` was run without naming a target adapter, and no project-root `emery.toml` carries a `[target]` table | `emery build <adapter>`, or add a `[target]` table to `emery.toml` and `emery build --config` |
| `adapter-reference` | An adapter was named without a version or a namespace, or as a path or bare name | Name the exact package reference, `namespace:name@version` |
| `unsupported-version` | The installed binary is older than an adapter's declared minimum `emery-version` | Reinstall through the binary's install channel (the hint prints the literal command), then rerun |
| `spec-not-generated` | `emery show` or `emery build` was run before any revision was committed | `/emery:specify <adapter>` first |
| `refused` | The loader rejected the release (an invalid or pre-compiled artifact, a `digest` it does not resolve to, or a namespace the binary routes nowhere) | Read the message; for an unrouted namespace, `wkg get <reference> -o ~/.emery/adapters/` as the hint says |
| `unavailable` | The registry fetch for a release the store lacks failed (network, endpoint, or a missing exact version) | Check connectivity and that the exact version is published under its namespace, then rerun; a copy at `~/.emery/adapters/<namespace>_<name>@<version>.wasm` stands in |
| `spec-outdated` | The stored revision was written under an older grammar than this binary reads | Re-run `/emery:specify <adapter>` to regenerate |

### Reviewing the generated documents

- Read `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` as re-projected after the run, or run `emery show spec` / `emery show design` / `emery show plan` — the document renders to stdout with its revision id in the front matter
- `spec.md`, `design.md`, and `plan.md` are projections of the stored revision; none is edited by hand — change a source and re-run `/emery:specify`

